    <div class="article-main__content" data-test-id="publishing-text-block">
        <h3><span class>1. Introduction and First Impressions (00:00:02)</span><!----></h3>
    </div>
  
<!----><!---->                  

    <div class="article-main__content" data-test-id="publishing-text-block">
        <p>
          <!----><br>
        </p>
    </div>
  
<!----><!---->                  

    <div class="article-main__content" data-test-id="publishing-text-block">
        <p>
          <span class="font-[700]">Alexandra:</span><span class> Hello and welcome to this new episode of Small Talk by Avanscoperta. I am Alexandra, and today we'll explore the topic </span><span class="font-[700]">"My flow with AI assistants"</span><span class> with our guest, </span><span class="font-[700]">Uberto Barbini</span><span class>, and our moderator, </span><span class="font-[700]">Alberto Brandolini</span><span class>. Hello, guys! Hi Alexandra, hi Alberto. Thanks both for accepting the invitation.</span><!---->
        </p>
    </div>
  
<!----><!---->                  

    <div class="article-main__content" data-test-id="publishing-text-block">
        <p>
          <span class>Just a quick reminder: we are live-streaming on YouTube and LinkedIn, so you can interact with Uberto and Alberto during the conversation using the chat. I'll pass it to Alberto now, so enjoy!</span><!---->
        </p>
    </div>
  
<!----><!---->                  

    <div class="article-main__content" data-test-id="publishing-text-block">
        <p>
          <span class="font-[700]">Alberto Brandolini:</span><span class> Hello everybody, thanks for joining. Hi Uberto, it's a pleasure to host you. I'd like to get straight to the first question. You’ve spent the last months working with AI, exploring new flows, and working on your new book about the topic. What was the most interesting discovery for you in your new habit, which changed how you use AI as a software professional?</span><!---->
        </p>
    </div>
  
<!----><!---->                  

    <div class="article-main__content" data-test-id="publishing-text-block">
        <p>
          <span class="font-[700]">Uberto Barbini:</span><span class> Thank you for the question. I started using ChatGPT several years ago, and then there was a period where everything was about visuals, like Midjourney and stuff like that. But the kind of "wow" feeling—the things that really made me change—was when I first used </span><span class="font-[700]">Cursor</span><span class> in January of last year, January 2024. At that time, it was still a bit rough, but it was already kind of amazing for many, many things.</span><!---->
        </p>
    </div>
  
<!----><!---->                  

    <div class="article-main__content" data-test-id="publishing-text-block">
        <p>
          <span class>I kept using it a bit more, and since last autumn, it's roughly one year that I've been using it basically every day. Since it was also a period between jobs, I had a lot of time to do my own projects and use this kind of assistant and </span><span class="font-[700]">Vibe Coding</span><span class> (before we had the Vibe Coding world). Now that I'm again working with a big corporate code base and everything, I'm still quite happy because I can still use it basically every day. It's actually a completely different way to use it when you are on a big code base, following customer-driven stuff—where they want the button brown and want to change or add a column—versus when you are </span><span class="font-[700]">Vibe Coding</span><span class> your next product for a future startup, where you just say, "Yeah, I want this feature." They are both very useful, but in completely different ways.</span><!---->
        </p>
    </div>
  
<!----><!---->                  

    <div class="article-main__content" data-test-id="publishing-text-block">
        <p>
          <!----><br>
        </p>
    </div>
  
<!----><!---->                  

    <div class="article-main__content" data-test-id="publishing-text-block">
        <h3><span class>2. The Need for Discipline and Simple Design Principles (00:04:01)</span><!----></h3>
    </div>
  
<!----><!---->                  

    <div class="article-main__content" data-test-id="publishing-text-block">
        <p>
          <!----><br>
        </p>
    </div>
  
<!----><!---->                  

    <div class="article-main__content" data-test-id="publishing-text-block">
        <p>
          <span class="font-[700]">Alberto Brandolini:</span><span class> I remember reading your manuscript. One of the things that was striking to me was the many references to </span><span class="font-[700]">Kent Beck</span><span class> and the </span><span class="font-[700]">TDD style and principles of simple design</span><span class>. I got the impression that this really needs a lot of discipline. It’s a place full of temptation, and you have to be something like a </span><span class="font-[700]">Buddhist monk</span><span class> to only pick the things that you need; otherwise, you could get completely out of control. That was interesting because it was a very mature attitude in a very interesting field. Did I get it wrong?</span><!---->
        </p>
    </div>
  
<!----><!---->                  

    <div class="article-main__content" data-test-id="publishing-text-block">
        <p>
          <span class="font-[700]">Uberto Barbini:</span><span class> No, you didn't. Just a bit of context: I started writing this book a few months ago. It's almost complete and will be out with the Pragmatic Programmers in October. It will be a small book, like a booklet of 100 pages, with a beta first.</span><!---->
        </p>
    </div>
  
<!----><!---->                  

    <div class="article-main__content" data-test-id="publishing-text-block">
        <p>
          <span class>Yes, this is one of my bigger insights. In the book, I have this image that </span><span class="font-[700]">when you learn to ski, you need to keep the edge</span><span class>, especially if you have a steep track. Otherwise, if the ski drives you, it can be very fast, but it will be very short. That's the kind of thing that happens, because using the AI assistant heavily gives you, at some point, a mix. First, there is this </span><span class="font-[700]">false feeling of security</span><span class>, and then there is also a bit of </span><span class="font-[700]">laziness</span><span class>. At some point, you start to think, "Okay, no, I don't really need to read the code, I don't really need to use my brain. The assistant said that everything is okay, so why run the test?"</span><!---->
        </p>
    </div>
  
<!----><!---->                  

    <div class="article-main__content" data-test-id="publishing-text-block">
        <p>
          <span class="font-[700]">Alberto Brandolini:</span><span class> It’s like the AI is </span><span class="font-[700]">sticking with the wrong side of your brain</span><span class>.</span><!---->
        </p>
    </div>
  
<!----><!---->                  

    <div class="article-main__content" data-test-id="publishing-text-block">
        <p>
          <span class="font-[700]">Uberto Barbini:</span><span class> Exactly! </span><span class="font-[700]">"You are a genius, so you don't need (to check)."</span><span class> This goes very bad. If I'm in some conversations, I'm like the super AI guy, because I use AI every day, and I think you can write great code with AI, and I think it's really boosting productivity. But on the other hand, when I'm talking with AI enthusiasts, I'm totally skeptical about the idea of AI "intelligence" for things that are called </span><span class="font-[700]">LLMs</span><span class> (Large Language Models)—it's just marketing in the end. You know the false dichotomy fallacy joke: "false dichotomies are bad but we need to accept them, because the alternative is cannibalism". </span><!---->
        </p>
    </div>
  
<!----><!---->                  

    <div class="article-main__content" data-test-id="publishing-text-block">
        <p>
          <span class="font-[700]">Alberto Brandolini:</span><span class> It’s actually hard to get any clear perspective. That's a place where there are too many voices, and I really try to understand what the skin in the game is behind the person talking about something. You might have people saying, "AI is a revolution," and then they're actually making money out of it. I'm not surprised.</span><!---->
        </p>
    </div>
  
<!----><!---->                  

    <div class="article-main__content" data-test-id="publishing-text-block">
        <p>
          <span class="font-[700]">Uberto Barbini:</span><span class> The amount of money is </span><span class="font-[700]">mind-blowing</span><span class>. Even in the most crazy days of the internet bubble, there was nothing near this: hundreds of billions, trillions. This is completely out of scale. So, it's hard to get the signal from the noise in this space.</span><!---->
        </p>
    </div>
  
<!----><!---->                  

    <div class="article-main__content" data-test-id="publishing-text-block">
        <p>
          <!----><br>
        </p>
    </div>
  
<!----><!---->                  

    <div class="article-main__content" data-test-id="publishing-text-block">
        <h3><span class>3. The Signal: Focusing on Boring Tasks and Ownership (00:09:13)</span><!----></h3>
    </div>
  
<!----><!---->                  

    <div class="article-main__content" data-test-id="publishing-text-block">
        <p>
          <!----><br>
        </p>
    </div>
  
<!----><!---->                  

    <div class="article-main__content" data-test-id="publishing-text-block">
        <p>
          <span class="font-[700]">Alberto Brandolini:</span><span class> Let's narrow it down. For a person in your role, position, and seniority, what is the </span><span class="font-[700]">signal</span><span class>? What is the structure or the change of habit that makes you more productive, efficient, or effective?</span><!---->
        </p>
    </div>
  
<!----><!---->                  

    <div class="article-main__content" data-test-id="publishing-text-block">
        <p>
          <span class="font-[700]">Uberto Barbini:</span><span class> A friend of mine summarized it: </span><span class="font-[700]">the more a task is boring, the more AI is useful</span><span class>. So, on one side, I think there is no reason now to do boring stuff. If I have a ticket that says, "change the column, add the field, change a bit of this and that," this is stuff that AI can definitely finish. You can go and </span><span class="font-[700]">prepare a coffee or answer a few emails</span><span class>.</span><!---->
        </p>
    </div>
  
<!----><!---->                  

    <div class="article-main__content" data-test-id="publishing-text-block">
        <p>
          <span class>But when it’s something that you actually need to use your brain for—when you want to explore a new design or lay the foundation of new stuff—you definitely need to know what you are doing.</span><!---->
        </p>
    </div>
  
<!----><!---->                  

    <div class="article-main__content" data-test-id="publishing-text-block">
        <p>
          <span class>The other point, especially talking from a professional developer's point of view, is that the current AI is definitely not able to work with a bigger code base and produce what you want. But even if it were possible, </span><span class="font-[700]">you really don't want to be kind of out of the loop</span><span class>. If you don't know what the AI did and why it did that, why are people paying you? You are cutting yourself out of your job, because the main point of a developer is not to </span><span class="font-[700]">tip-tap the code</span><span class>; it's being paid because you know the domain and you know how the code is structured, so you can reason, estimate, and have a discussion. If you do stuff completely blindly, the code is terrible, and they will probably fire you anyway, but even if they don't, you basically have no idea what's going on.</span><!---->
        </p>
    </div>
  
<!----><!---->                  

    <div class="article-main__content" data-test-id="publishing-text-block">
        <p>
          <span class="font-[700]">Alberto Brandolini:</span><span class> I remember there was one line that was about responsibility: </span><span class="font-[700]">"You own it, you run it."</span><span class> Senior developers know that this means getting through the maintenance board, ownership, and responsibility, and trying to understand what is written. That's a space that might help us understand how to control how much speed to use.</span><!---->
        </p>
    </div>
  
<!----><!---->                  

    <div class="article-main__content" data-test-id="publishing-text-block">
        <p>
          <span class="font-[700]">Uberto Barbini:</span><span class> Yes, and this is also true for the opposite side. For stuff that you really don't care about—it's just a proof of concept you want to show—you can definitely go with </span><span class="font-[700]">Vibe Coding</span><span class>, not looking at the code. With a small code base that the LLM builds itself, it more or less follows what you think are the good principles (or the principles that it has been trained on). It is definitely able to progress very quickly. You can do something that would take one week in a few hours, and that's also a lot of time saved.</span><!---->
        </p>
    </div>
  
<!----><!---->                  

    <div class="article-main__content" data-test-id="publishing-text-block">
        <p>
          <!----><br>
        </p>
    </div>
  
<!----><!---->                  

    <div class="article-main__content" data-test-id="publishing-text-block">
        <h3><span class>4. Strategies for Large Codebases and Bounded Contexts (00:13:48)</span><!----></h3>
    </div>
  
<!----><!---->                  

    <div class="article-main__content" data-test-id="publishing-text-block">
        <p>
          <!----><br>
        </p>
    </div>
  
<!----><!---->                  

    <div class="article-main__content" data-test-id="publishing-text-block">
        <p>
          <span class="font-[700]">Alberto Brandolini:</span><span class> You mentioned very large projects and code bases. This is where AI and LLMs start reducing their effectiveness. Is there any trick or strategy that we can play? I'm thinking of </span><span class="font-[700]">Domain-Driven Design (DDD)</span><span class>, bounded contexts, or documentation.</span><!---->
        </p>
    </div>
  
<!----><!---->                  

    <div class="article-main__content" data-test-id="publishing-text-block">
        <p>
          <span class="font-[700]">Uberto Barbini:</span><span class> You know how the LLMs work: they just try to progress based on whatever is in the prompt. They really cannot keep the whole big code base in their mind. So, they need a lot of </span><span class="font-[700]">documents to support them</span><span class>—documents that can be like the stepping stone before they start writing code. If you have a document that says, "We want to write code in this way, the design is based on this," you can even let the LLM produce the document, but you still need to review and make sure it makes sense.</span><!---->
        </p>
    </div>
  
<!----><!---->                  

    <div class="article-main__content" data-test-id="publishing-text-block">
        <p>
          <span class="font-[700]">Domain-Driven Design is actually pretty good</span><span class> because, even if people don't realize it, LLMs are easily misled by hard-to-read code. In that sense, they are similar to humans. They don't care probably about the name of variables or methods much, but if the code is confusing, they also get confused very quickly. If the code has </span><span class="font-[700]">meaningful names</span><span class> that are kept consistent across the whole codebase, that's very useful.</span><!---->
        </p>
    </div>
  
<!----><!---->                  

    <div class="article-main__content" data-test-id="publishing-text-block">
        <p>
          <span class>For example, one typical small thing: our codebase has a lot of customizations for each country. One of the typical things LLMs do is they pick up the </span><span class="font-[700]">wrong context</span><span class>. You say, "I want to change this client attribute," and it tries to change it on the Japanese client class, which should only have the override or stuff like that. That's because they just reason with the names and use search-like functions. So, if you start from scratch or you have a very good naming convention, that's definitely something that helps.</span><!---->
        </p>
    </div>
  
                    
    

