    <div class="article-main__content" data-test-id="publishing-text-block">
        <h3><span class>5. Team Practices and Tool Set (00:18:43)</span><!----></h3>
    </div>
  
<!----><!---->                  

    <div class="article-main__content" data-test-id="publishing-text-block">
        <p>
          <!----><br>
        </p>
    </div>
  
<!----><!---->                  

    <div class="article-main__content" data-test-id="publishing-text-block">
        <p>
          <span class="font-[700]">Alberto Brandolini:</span><span class> I saw a question from the list: apart from individual productivity, how did you change your team's practices to multiply the overall effectiveness?</span><!---->
        </p>
    </div>
  
<!----><!---->                  

    <div class="article-main__content" data-test-id="publishing-text-block">
        <p>
          <span class="font-[700]">Uberto Barbini:</span><span class> AI can also be used, apart from coding, for things like writing </span><span class="font-[700]">documentation</span><span class> and getting </span><span class="font-[700]">minutes for meetings automatically</span><span class>. There is a lot of use in general project management where AI is useful. But keeping it to coding, there's not much, to be honest. The only thing that is important is to keep the </span><span class="font-[700]">rule set documented</span><span class>. You want to specify how to write the code, the code convention, how to write the test first, or "don't put stupid comments that just rewrite what is in the code." Keep these rules in the </span><span class="font-[700]">shared repository</span><span class> so they can be updated by everybody and kept in sync.</span><!---->
        </p>
    </div>
  
<!----><!---->                  

    <div class="article-main__content" data-test-id="publishing-text-block">
        <p>
          <span class>Apart from that, it's more like an effect of some tasks that used to be longer taking a much shorter time. For example, making a few hundred items translatable. AI can do that in 20 minutes, which would otherwise be a two- or three-day task for a human, because people get bored. Other tasks actually don't get any faster at all. There is also a bit of a risk that people start using AI even when it's not helping—just trying to avoid work and going into a loop where actually, if you write the code, it would be much faster.</span><!---->
        </p>
    </div>
  
<!----><!---->                  

    <div class="article-main__content" data-test-id="publishing-text-block">
        <p>
          <span class="font-[700]">Alberto Brandolini:</span><span class> Then you get into a psychological battle somehow.</span><!---->
        </p>
    </div>
  
<!----><!---->                  

    <div class="article-main__content" data-test-id="publishing-text-block">
        <p>
          <span class="font-[700]">Alberto Brandolini:</span><span class> I see that in the chat, there are two similar questions about which assistants/agents you use, and if you used any specific access to do specific tasks. It feels like a kid in a toy shop—which one should I start with?</span><!---->
        </p>
    </div>
  
<!----><!---->                  

    <div class="article-main__content" data-test-id="publishing-text-block">
        <p>
          <span class="font-[700]">Uberto Barbini:</span><span class> It's very different. For new stuff, like </span><span class="font-[700]">Vibe Coding</span><span class>, I really like </span><span class="font-[700]">OpenCode</span><span class> (the open-source one), or </span><span class="font-[700]">Cloud Code</span><span class> (the famous one). These are command-line tools, and they are much more suited to Vibe Coding. You almost never look at the code, so there is no point in having an IDE, because 90% of the IDE stuff you don't care about. That's very effective.</span><!---->
        </p>
    </div>
  
<!----><!---->                  

    <div class="article-main__content" data-test-id="publishing-text-block">
        <p>
          <span class>Instead, to work in a bigger code base, </span><span class="font-[700]">Cursor</span><span class> is probably my favorite at this moment. I use </span><span class="font-[700]">GitHub Copilot</span><span class> and the </span><span class="font-[700]">JetBrains</span><span class> one (JetBrains AI). The JetBrains tool, for example, is particularly good at </span><span class="font-[700]">understanding the code</span><span class>. When I joined a new company, LLMs were really, really useful in the </span><span class="font-[700]">onboarding process</span><span class>. I was able to be kind of productive in a few weeks instead of a few months. That was good for the company, and it was also good for me personally because there was much less stress when you don't know how to do anything in a bigger code base and you need to ask and wait for people—it’s a bit frustrating. The JetBrains tool is probably the best one for </span><span class="font-[700]">extracting information</span><span class>, but it's not always great at writing code, so I switch models and tools depending on the task.</span><!---->
        </p>
    </div>
  
<!----><!---->                  

    <div class="article-main__content" data-test-id="publishing-text-block">
        <p>
          <span class>It's also hard to be certain, because every time there is a new version, things change slightly. </span><span class="font-[700]">Clause 4</span><span class> used to be my favorite, but </span><span class="font-[700]">GPT-5</span><span class> is my favorite now. The former tended to do too much. I mean, I would ask it to put a button, and it would write 25 new rules and stuff.</span><!---->
        </p>
    </div>
  
<!----><!---->                  

    <div class="article-main__content" data-test-id="publishing-text-block">
        <p>
          <span class="font-[700]">Alberto Brandolini:</span><span class> </span><span class="font-[700]">Over-zealous</span><span class>!</span><!---->
        </p>
    </div>
  
<!----><!---->                  

    <div class="article-main__content" data-test-id="publishing-text-block">
