<!----><!---->                  

    <div class="article-main__content" data-test-id="publishing-text-block">
        <h3><span class>6. Iterative Interaction and Role-Playing (00:25:38)</span><!----></h3>
    </div>
  
<!----><!---->                  

    <div class="article-main__content" data-test-id="publishing-text-block">
        <p>
          <!----><br>
        </p>
    </div>
  
<!----><!---->                  

    <div class="article-main__content" data-test-id="publishing-text-block">
        <p>
          <span class="font-[700]">Alberto Brandolini:</span><span class> I noticed a few other things that were interesting: the way you interact with AI. Sometimes it's like the knight riding a horse, and you're telling the horse where to go. A lot of times, you flip the situation, and the AI gives you suggestions about what to do, or maybe gives you a plan. Any thoughts on this iterative experimentation?</span><!---->
        </p>
    </div>
  
<!----><!---->                  

    <div class="article-main__content" data-test-id="publishing-text-block">
        <p>
          <span class="font-[700]">Uberto Barbini:</span><span class> This is actually pretty interesting. If you need to do something that you really don't know how to do because you don't know the code base or stuff like that, it's really not a good idea to ask the AI to write the code. It probably won't work, and even if it works, you wouldn't understand anything. The usual situation is that it kind of works, but then in the code review, people ask, "Why are you doing this stupid thing?" and you don't even know that it was a stupid thing because you don't really understand the code.</span><!---->
        </p>
    </div>
  
<!----><!---->                  

    <div class="article-main__content" data-test-id="publishing-text-block">
        <p>
          <span class>In this case, the better way is to </span><span class="font-[700]">ask the AI, "What should I do to do this?"</span><span class> and then try to implement it yourself, or tell the AI to do it but in small pieces. It's pretty good to </span><span class="font-[700]">discuss with the AI</span><span class>, and you can keep asking questions, "Why should I use this?" or "Why should I use that?" You look at the code and you say, "Actually, the code seems to do something different."</span><!---->
        </p>
    </div>
  
<!----><!---->                  

    <div class="article-main__content" data-test-id="publishing-text-block">
        <p>
          <span class>Sometimes it is quite correct. I was also switching languages; this current project is in Python, and I knew Python, but not like the Python used in a big code base. There were a lot of things that I didn't know, and sometimes I was thinking, "Oh, this stuff seems wrong," and then it would explain, "No, in Python you actually need to do this before." It's a good way to </span><span class="font-[700]">challenge the AI</span><span class>.</span><!---->
        </p>
    </div>
  
<!----><!---->                  

    <div class="article-main__content" data-test-id="publishing-text-block">
        <p>
          <span class>Just another real example: I was doing some changes in the UI, and then there were some tests failing. I asked the AI, "Can you fix this test, just to make it pass?" What the AI did was, since it had the rule, "Don't change the assertion in the test," which is usually a good rule, it basically </span><span class="font-[700]">rolled back all the changes in the UI</span><span class> so the test was passing again! This is the kind of stupidity that not even a super junior developer would do, because they just use the context. I had to say, "No, put everything back and change the assertion—in this case, it is okay to change."</span><!---->
        </p>
    </div>
  
<!----><!---->                  

    <div class="article-main__content" data-test-id="publishing-text-block">
        <p>
          <span class="font-[700]">Alberto Brandolini:</span><span class> You also used a lot of </span><span class="font-[700]">role-playing</span><span class> configuration, like "talk to me like an expert" or "talk to me like a junior."</span><!---->
        </p>
    </div>
  
<!----><!---->                  

    <div class="article-main__content" data-test-id="publishing-text-block">
        <p>
          <span class="font-[700]">Uberto Barbini:</span><span class> Yes, but I don't like very much just saying, "Talk like an expert." It's not that it writes better code. It just </span><span class="font-[700]">changes the attitude</span><span class> of the LLM, so it becomes arrogant and not useful. But if you say, </span><span class="font-[700]">"Write the code following DDD principles"</span><span class> or </span><span class="font-[700]">"following Kent Beck principles,"</span><span class> that definitely works. They have this "chain of thought" feature now, where they write the code, then take the written code as input, and check again if it was matching what you asked for.</span><!---->
        </p>
    </div>
  
<!----><!---->                  

    <div class="article-main__content" data-test-id="publishing-text-block">
        <p>
